{
  "id": "plural",
  "name": "Plural nouns",
  "level": "A1",
  "subtitle": "Most nouns add -s for the plural: cat, cats. After s, x, ch or sh add -es: box, boxes. Some nouns are irregular: child, children.",
  "briefMarks": ["-s", "-es", "irregular"],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "one noun",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ -s / -es / -ies",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "= more than one"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "a cat → two cat<span class=\"vp-verb\">s</span> · a box → two box<span class=\"vp-verb\">es</span> · a baby → two bab<span class=\"vp-verb\">ies</span> · a child → two child<span class=\"vp-verb\">ren</span>",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Most nouns just add <strong>-s</strong>: <em>a book → two books, a day → two days, a dog → three dogs</em>.",
        "tag": "-s"
      },
      {
        "html": "After <strong>s, x, ch, sh</strong> add <strong>-es</strong>: <em>a bus → buses, a box → boxes, a watch → watches, a dish → dishes</em>. You hear an extra syllable. Many nouns that end in <em>-o</em> also take <em>-es</em>: <em>tomatoes, potatoes</em> (but <em>photos, pianos</em>).",
        "tag": "-es"
      },
      {
        "html": "Consonant + <strong>y</strong> becomes <strong>-ies</strong>: <em>a baby → babies, a city → cities</em>. Vowel + y just adds -s: <em>a day → days, a boy → boys</em>. Many nouns that end in <em>-f / -fe</em> change to <strong>-ves</strong>: <em>a knife → knives, a leaf → leaves</em>.",
        "tag": "-ies · -ves"
      },
      {
        "html": "Some plurals are <strong>irregular</strong>. Learn them one by one: <em>man → men, woman → women, child → children, person → people, foot → feet, tooth → teeth, mouse → mice</em>. Not: <em>childs, mans, persons</em>. Some nouns do not change: <em>one sheep, two sheep; one fish, two fish</em>.",
        "tag": "irregular"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Uncountable</strong> nouns have no plural: <em>information, advice, furniture, news</em>. Say <em>some information</em>, not <em>informations</em>. Some nouns are always plural: <em>trousers, glasses, scissors</em>.",
        "tag": "no plural"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "After a plural noun the verb is <em>are</em>, not <em>is</em>: <em>The children are happy.</em> You cannot use <em>a</em> before a plural: <em>a book</em>, but just <em>books</em>. More: <a href=\"/articles\">Articles: a, an, the</a> and <a href=\"/nouns\">Nouns: people, things, places</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
